<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<worksheet xml:id="Math234Sp22-Midterm1" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title>Math 234 - Midterm 1 - Spring 2022</title>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Let <m>\vec{a}=\hat{\imath}-2 \hat{k}</m> and <m>\vec{b}=2 \hat{\imath}+3 \hat{\jmath}-\hat{k}</m>.
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Are the vectors <m>\vec{a}</m> and <m>\vec{b}</m> orthogonal?
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\vec{a} \cdot \vec{b} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ -2 \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 3 \\ -1 \end{pmatrix} = 2 + 0 + 2 = 4 \neq 0.
					</me>
					No, they are not orthogonal.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find the area of the triangle with corners at the origin, the point <m>(1,0,-2)</m>, and the point <m>(2,3,-1)</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					The area of the triangle is <m>\frac{1}{2}|\vec{a} \times \vec{b}|</m>.
					<me>
						\vec{a} \times \vec{b} = \begin{vmatrix} \hat{\imath} \amp \hat{\jmath} \amp \hat{k} \\ 1 \amp 0 \amp -2 \\ 2 \amp 3 \amp -1 \end{vmatrix} = \langle 6, -3, 3 \rangle.
					</me>
					The area is <m>\frac{1}{2}\sqrt{6^2 + (-3)^2 + 3^2} = \frac{1}{2}\sqrt{54} = \frac{3\sqrt{6}}{2}</m>.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find the equation of the plane that contains the vectors <m>\vec{a}</m> and <m>\vec{b}</m> and that passes through the point <m>(0,2,3)</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					The normal vector of the plane is <m>\vec{n} = \vec{a} \times \vec{b} = \langle 6, -3, 3 \rangle</m>.
					The equation of the plane is:
					<me>
						6(x-0) - 3(y-2) + 3(z-3) = 0.
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<statement>
			<p>
				An object's acceleration is given by
				<me>\vec{a}(t)=\langle t+2,\cos (t),t^2+1\rangle.</me>
				Find a vector equation <m>\vec{r}(t)</m> for the position of the object at time <m>t</m> if its initial velocity is <m>\vec{v}(0)=\langle 0,1,2 \rangle</m> and its initial position is <m>\vec{r}(0)=\langle 1,0,0 \rangle</m>.
			</p>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<p>
				<me>
					\begin{aligned}
					\vec{v}(t) \amp = \int \vec{a}(t) \, dt = \langle \frac{t^2}{2} + 2t + c_1, \sin t + c_2, \frac{t^3}{3} + t + c_3 \rangle \\
					\vec{v}(0) \amp = \langle 0, 1, 2 \rangle \implies c_1 = 0, c_2 = 1, c_3 = 2 \\
					\vec{v}(t) \amp = \langle \frac{t^2}{2} + 2t, \sin t + 1, \frac{t^3}{3} + t + 2 \rangle
					\end{aligned}
				</me>
				<me>
					\begin{aligned}
					\vec{r}(t) \amp = \int \vec{v}(t) \, dt = \langle \frac{t^3}{6} + t^2 + D_1, -\cos t + t + D_2, \frac{t^4}{12} + \frac{t^2}{2} + 2t + D_3 \rangle \\
					\vec{r}(0) \amp = \langle 1, 0, 0 \rangle = \langle D_1, -1 + D_2, D_3 \rangle \implies D_1 = 1, D_2 = 1, D_3 = 0 \\
					\vec{r}(t) \amp = \langle \frac{t^3}{6} + t^2 + 1, -\cos t + t + 1, \frac{t^4}{12} + \frac{t^2}{2} + 2t \rangle
					\end{aligned}
				</me>
			</p>
		</solution>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Two particles begin moving at time <m>t=0</m> seconds. Particle <m>P_1</m> has path given by <m>\vec{r}_1(t)=\langle 2 t^2, t+1,5\rangle</m>. Particle <m>P_2</m> has path given by <m>\vec{r}_2(t)=\langle t^2-t-18, t-3, t\rangle</m>. The paths of these two particles intersect at some point <m>Q</m>.
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find point <m>Q</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Let <m>t</m> be the parameter for <m>P_1</m> and <m>s</m> for <m>P_2</m>.
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						2t^2 \amp = s^2 - s - 18 \\
						t+1 \amp = s-3 \\
						5 \amp = s
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
					From the third coordinate, <m>s = 5</m>.
					Substituting into the second equation: <m>t+1 = 5-3 \implies t = 1</m>.
					Checking the first equation: <m>2(1)^2 = 2</m> and <m>5^2 - 5 - 18 = 25 - 23 = 2</m>.
					The point <m>Q</m> is <m>\vec{r}_1(1) = \langle 2, 2, 5 \rangle</m>.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Do the particles collide at <m>Q</m>? If so, show why. If not, which arrives at <m>Q</m> first?
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					No, they do not collide because they arrive at <m>Q</m> at different times (<m>t=1</m> for <m>P_1</m> and <m>t=5</m> for <m>P_2</m>).
					<m>P_1</m> arrives at <m>Q</m> first.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Which particle is traveling faster when it arrives at <m>Q</m>?
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						\vec{v}_1(t) \amp = \langle 4t, 1, 0 \rangle, \quad |\vec{v}_1(1)| = \sqrt{16+1} = \sqrt{17} \\
						\vec{v}_2(t) \amp = \langle 2t-1, 1, 1 \rangle, \quad |\vec{v}_2(5)| = \sqrt{9^2+1^2+1^2} = \sqrt{83}
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
					<m>P_2</m> is traveling faster.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Show that the acceleration vectors for both particles are parallel.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						\vec{a}_1(t) \amp = \langle 4, 0, 0 \rangle \\
						\vec{a}_2(t) \amp = \langle 2, 0, 0 \rangle
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
					Since <m>\vec{a}_1(t) = 2 \vec{a}_2(t)</m>, the vectors are parallel.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Let <m>\vec{r}(t)=\langle e^{4 t^2}, 9 t, 5 t^2+1\rangle</m>.
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Write down an integral that represents the length of the curve, with <m>0 \leq t \leq 2</m>. You do NOT need to solve the integral.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						\vec{r}\,'(t) \amp = \langle 8t e^{4t^2}, 9, 10t \rangle \\
						L \amp = \int_0^2 \sqrt{(8t e^{4t^2})^2 + 81 + 100t^2} \, dt
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find a parametric equation for the tangent line to the curve at <m>t=1</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					The point is <m>\vec{r}(1) = \langle e^4, 9, 6 \rangle</m>.
					The direction vector is <m>\vec{r}\,'(1) = \langle 8e^4, 9, 10 \rangle</m>.
					The tangent line is:
					<me>
						\begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} e^4 \\ 9 \\ 6 \end{pmatrix} + t \begin{pmatrix} 8e^4 \\ 9 \\ 10 \end{pmatrix}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Let <m>\vec{r}(t)=\langle 4 \cos (t), t, 4 \sin (t)\rangle</m>.
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find the unit tangent vector, <m>\vec{T}(t)</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						\vec{r}\,'(t) \amp = \langle -4 \sin t, 1, 4 \cos t \rangle \\
						|\vec{r}\,'(t)| \amp = \sqrt{16 \sin^2 t + 1 + 16 \cos^2 t} = \sqrt{17} \\
						\vec{T}(t) \amp = \langle -\frac{4}{\sqrt{17}} \sin t, \frac{1}{\sqrt{17}}, \frac{4}{\sqrt{17}} \cos t \rangle
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find the unit normal vector, <m>\vec{N}(t)</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						\vec{T}\,'(t) \amp = \langle -\frac{4}{\sqrt{17}} \cos t, 0, -\frac{4}{\sqrt{17}} \sin t \rangle \\
						|\vec{T}\,'(t)| \amp = \sqrt{\frac{16}{17} \cos^2 t + \frac{16}{17} \sin^2 t} = \frac{4}{\sqrt{17}} \\
						\vec{N}(t) \amp = \langle -\cos t, 0, -\sin t \rangle
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find the curvature, <m>\kappa(t)</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>\kappa = \frac{|\vec{T}\,'(t)|}{|\vec{r}\,'(t)|} = \frac{4/\sqrt{17}}{\sqrt{17}} = \frac{4}{17}.</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Find a vector function that parametrizes the curve of intersection of the surfaces <m>y=-x^2+2 z</m> and <m>x^2+z^2=16</m>.
			</p>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<p>
				Let <m>x = 4 \sin t</m> and <m>z = 4 \cos t</m>. Then <m>x^2 + z^2 = 16</m>.
				Substitute into the first surface:
				<me>y = -(4 \sin t)^2 + 2(4 \cos t) = -16 \sin^2 t + 8 \cos t.</me>
				The parametrization is:
				<me>\vec{r}(t) = \langle 4 \sin t, -16 \sin^2 t + 8 \cos t, 4 \cos t \rangle.</me>
			</p>
		</solution>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Consider the function <m>f(x, y)=\sqrt{y-x^2}</m>.
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Sketch the domain of the function. Shade the region(s) that are part of the domain. Use dotted lines when sketching a curve that is NOT in the domain, and solid lines when sketching curves that are.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					We need <m>y - x^2 \ge 0 \implies y \ge x^2</m>.
					The domain is the region on and above the parabola <m>y = x^2</m>.
					The parabola is included (solid line).
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Sketch the level curves of <m>f(x, y)</m> for <m>k=0,1,2</m> (there should be three curves total). Label each curve with the appropriate value of <m>k</m> and indicate in your sketch the <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> intercepts for the curves (if they exist).
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						k \amp = 0 \implies \sqrt{y-x^2} = 0 \implies y = x^2 \\
						k \amp = 1 \implies \sqrt{y-x^2} = 1 \implies y = x^2 + 1 \\
						k \amp = 2 \implies \sqrt{y-x^2} = 2 \implies y = x^2 + 4
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Let <m>f(x, y)=\frac{4 x y^3}{2 x^2 y^2+5 y^4}</m>. Show that <m>\lim _{(x, y) \rightarrow(0,0)} f(x, y)</m> does not exist.
			</p>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<p>
				Along Path 1 <m>y = x</m>:
				<me>\lim_{x \to 0} \frac{4x^4}{2x^4 + 5x^4} = \frac{4}{7}.</me>
				Along Path 2 <m>y = 2x</m>:
				<me>\lim_{x \to 0} \frac{4x(8x^3)}{2x^2(4x^2) + 5(16x^4)} = \frac{32x^4}{88x^4} = \frac{4}{11}.</me>
				Since limits are different, the limit does not exist.
			</p>
		</solution>
	</exercise>
</worksheet>
